        "title": "refactor(active-memory): split runtime modules",
        "body": "Closes #106280\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe Active Memory plugin runtime had grown to 4,182 lines in one entrypoint, combining configuration, session eligibility, prompt/query shaping, transcript evidence, recall execution, and plugin registration. That made reviews slower and made ownership boundaries harder to see.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis keeps `index.ts` as the thin plugin registration surface and moves the existing runtime into focused sibling modules for types/constants, configuration, sessions, prompts/queries, transcript evidence, and recall execution. Runtime behavior, plugin exports, config/storage contracts, and the existing test helper surface remain unchanged. Every changed production module is now below the repository's 500-line LOC ratchet; the largest is 445 lines.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNo user-visible behavior change. Maintainers get smaller review surfaces and explicit one-way module dependencies for future Active Memory fixes.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `corepack pnpm test extensions/active-memory` — 179 tests passed on Blacksmith Testbox `tbx_01kxe08p2t4208d1hdse6cct5t` ([Actions run](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/29260856329)).\n- `corepack pnpm check:loc --base origin/main -- extensions/active-memory` — all 13 changed production files passed the 500-line ratchet on the same Testbox.\n- `corepack pnpm check:test-types` and `corepack pnpm build` — full test-type and build gates passed on the same Testbox.\n- Fresh autoreview of the final split: no findings; patch correct with 0.97 confidence.\n\nAI-assisted: yes. The implementation and generated diff were reviewed against the complete Active Memory test surface.\n",
